# File Editing

## NEVER use shell commands or scripts to modify code

This is the **hard top-tier rule** of this repo. **Never** use `sed`,
`awk`, `perl -i`, `tr`, `find -exec`, PowerShell `(Get-Content … | Set-Content)`,
heredoc-into-file, `echo > file`, or any other shell construct that mutates
source files. **Always** use the dedicated edit tools: `StrReplace`,
`Write`, or `EditNotebook`.

Why: shell-based rewrites bypass Cursor's diff UI. The user cannot review,
accept, or reject the change — it just happens silently. Even a "tiny
rename" across many files must be done as a series of `StrReplace` calls
(one per file, or `replace_all: true` within a file) so each edit shows up
in the diff panel.

- ❌ `sed -i 's/foo/bar/g' src/**/*.ts`
- ❌ `find src -name '*.ts' -exec sed -i …`
- ❌ `node -e "require('fs').writeFileSync(…)"`
- ❌ `cat > file.ts <<'EOF' … EOF`
- ✅ `StrReplace` with `replace_all: true` for in-file mass-rename
- ✅ One `StrReplace` per file for cross-file rename (read each first)
- ✅ `Write` only when creating a new file or wholesale rewriting
  (after `Read`)

The `Shell` tool is for actual terminal operations (`git`, `npm`,
`tsc`, `lint`, etc.) only. If you catch yourself reaching for `sed`
to "save tool calls", **stop** — the user must see every code change.

## ALWAYS read a file before editing it

The user may have edited the file since you last read it. Your memory of
the file is stale. Before **any** `Write` or `StrReplace` call, call
`Read` on the target path **in the same turn**.

- `Write` is **destructive** — no old_string check, no safety net. A
  stale mental model silently erases the user's edits.
- `StrReplace` self-validates via `old_string`, but when the fuzzy match
  shows the file has drifted from what you expect, **re-read first**
  rather than forcing a match.
- A `preToolUse` hook (see `.cursor/hooks.json`) blocks `Write` on
  existing files that have not been read this session. Do not work
  around it — call `Read` and retry.

## No legacy fallbacks

This is a greenfield codebase. Do not add compatibility shims, alternate
resolution paths, or "just in case" code. See **`no-legacy-fallbacks.mdc`**.
